channels, products and customer attributes.

The analysis identified several important data-quality limitations.

First, Ticket Type exactly duplicates the Category target and
Ticket Priority exactly duplicates the Urgency target. These
variables therefore represent target leakage and should not be
used as predictive features.

Second, the text data contains repeated and template-style
descriptions. Several Resolution values also contain noisy text
that does not consistently represent meaningful support outcomes.

Third, important operational fields contain substantial missing
values, particularly First Response Time and Time to Resolution.
There is also no ticket-created timestamp, which prevents reliable
calculation of response and resolution durations.

The text-based category classifier achieved an accuracy that did
not outperform the majority-class baseline. The satisfaction
prediction model similarly remained close to baseline performance.

Therefore, the dataset is more suitable for demonstrating:

• Data cleaning and preprocessing
• Exploratory data analysis
• Missing-value analysis
• Data-quality assessment
• Target leakage detection
• Statistical analysis
• NLP preprocessing
• ML feasibility assessment

rather than presenting a production-ready ticket classification
system.

The project demonstrates an important data-science principle:
a machine-learning model should only be trusted when the underlying
data contains reliable and meaningful predictive information.
